<Annex
  title="Where the Time Goes"
  piece="03"
  eyebrow="Builder's Annex · 03 · Performance"
  dek="One MLIP call has a characteristic cost profile, and it is not the one you tune an LLM for. Where the time goes, and the developments the field uses to move it."
  prevHref="/annex/the-machine"
  prevLabel="The Machine"
  nextHref="/annex/the-fine-tuning-problem"
  nextLabel="The Fine-Tuning Problem"
>
  <p>
    You serve an MLIP through one of two sockets. In Python it is the ASE <code>Calculator</code>: an
    object with <code>get_potential_energy</code> and <code>get_forces</code>, attached to an
    <code>Atoms</code> object, which is why Rootstock can serve every family behind one interface.
    For large parallel runs it is a LAMMPS pair style, where LAMMPS owns the atoms and the
    parallelism and calls the model as a plugin. Either way the caller is a loop, not a person, and
    it runs the model millions of times in a row. The number that matters is per-call latency inside
    that loop. Throughput on independent requests flatters the hardware and tells an MD user nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the whole game is one call, run a million times. Look at where its time goes.
  </p>

  <h2>The cost of one call</h2>

  <p>
    A call is a pure function from atoms to one energy, with the forces its gradient (that is the
    subject of the previous piece). The wall-clock breaks into five pieces, and only two of them are
    the network.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Neighbor list.</strong> Atoms interact directly only within a cutoff radius, 5 to 6
    ångströms in practice, so before anything else something must find each atom's neighbors inside
    that radius, typically 20 to 80 of them. Comparing every atom against every other scales with
    the square of the count; binning atoms into a grid and checking only adjacent cells scales
    linearly. The list is not rebuilt every step: pad the cutoff with a margin called the skin, and
    the list stays valid until some atom has moved half the skin distance. Amortized over steps it
    is cheap. Rebuild it every step by accident and you lose half your step budget, which is a
    common way to do exactly that.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Graph assembly.</strong> The neighbor list becomes a graph: atoms are nodes, neighbor
    pairs are edges, stored as two integer tensors of source and destination indices plus a per-edge
    shift vector recording which periodic image the neighbor sits in. Pure bookkeeping, but it runs
    every step and it crosses the CPU-to-GPU boundary.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Forward pass.</strong> One shape, across every family. Each atom's atomic number indexes
    an embedding table of about 100 rows, a vocabulary that feels like a rounding error after
    transformer vocabularies. Each edge is described by its length, expanded in a radial basis (a
    soft one-hot over distance), and its direction, expanded in spherical harmonics (a basis over
    orientations, and the reason these models respect rotation). Then a handful of interaction
    blocks, two to six rather than dozens, in which each atom gathers messages from its neighbors:
    tensor products combine the edge's directional features with the neighbor's features, small
    matrix multiplies mix channels, and a scatter-add sums the messages into the atom's new state. A
    small readout maps each atom's state to a per-atom energy, and a sum over atoms gives the scalar.
    SchNet uses distances only; NequIP carries directional features through the blocks; MACE builds
    higher-body-order products and so needs only about two blocks; EquiformerV2 and eSEN put
    attention inside each neighborhood. The skeleton is identical.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Backward pass.</strong> Autograd from the energy back to the positions gives the forces,
    at roughly the cost of the forward.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Integration.</strong> Updating positions and velocities is a handful of vector
    operations. Ignore it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice below that backward costs about as much as forward, and that the bookkeeping segments
    barely move when you optimize.
  </p>

