  <p>
    This is not a transformer's cost profile. A transformer forward is a few enormous matrix
    multiplies and the GPU stays busy. An MLIP forward is scatters, gathers, and many small matrix
    multiplies, because the tensors are sized by atoms and edges instead of by a 4,096-wide hidden
    dimension. Bespoke models run a hundred thousand to a million parameters; MACE-MP-0 medium is
    4.7 million; most foundation checkpoints stay under a hundred million active, though UMA-medium
    reaches 1.4 billion total with about 50 million active per structure. At a few hundred atoms the
    GPU spends much of each step idle, waiting for the CPU to launch the next tiny kernel. Memory
    traffic on the edges and kernel-launch overhead, not matmul throughput, are what a fast MLIP
    stack fights.
  </p>

  <h2>The developments that move it</h2>

  <p>
    Six techniques do most of the work in production, each aimed at one part of that profile. None is
    a free win; each trades something, and which ones matter depends on whether you are running one
    long trajectory, a farm of short relaxations, or a billion atoms across nodes.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Batch independent structures.</strong> Graphs compose block-diagonally.
    Concatenate the atoms of many structures into one tensor, offset each structure's edge indices,
    keep a vector recording which structure each atom belongs to, and one forward pass evaluates them
    all. Kernel launches amortize, and the small matrix multiplies become big ones. A relaxation farm
    of ten thousand candidate crystals, each stepping downhill, is the ideal customer. A single long
    MD trajectory is the worst: step one million and one depends on step one million, so it cannot
    batch with itself. Establish this asymmetry first about any workload you are handed to speed up.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Stop recompiling.</strong> <code>torch.compile</code> and CUDA graphs assume
    fixed tensor shapes, but the edge count changes every step as atoms drift in and out of each
    other's cutoff spheres. Naively every step is a new shape and a recompile, and the optimized path
    runs slower than eager. Pad to shape buckets: round the atom and edge counts up to the next of a
    handful of preset sizes, mask the padding, and the whole run compiles five times instead of once
    per step. The padding wastes a few percent of arithmetic, and the bucket cache earns it back many
    times over.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Fuse the hot kernel.</strong> The tensor product at the heart of the
    equivariant families is the Clebsch-Gordan product, which combines directional edge features with
    node features without breaking rotational symmetry. It decomposes into a swarm of tiny multiplies,
    one per allowed combination of angular channels, and in plain PyTorch that swarm is a swarm of
    kernel launches. Fused implementations do it in a few kernels; NVIDIA's cuEquivariance is the one
    to know. On NequIP- and MACE-shaped models this is often the single largest win at
    medium-to-large atom counts. At small counts, killing kernel-launch overhead with CUDA graphs
    matters more.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Pick precision by conservation, not static error.</strong> The default is
    float32. TF32 speeds up the matrix multiplies but injects noise into the forces, and the metric
    that catches the damage is not force error on a held-out set: it is whether the total energy of a
    closed simulation drifts over a million steps. Forces slightly inconsistent with the energy act
    like a hidden heater or refrigerator, invisible in any single step and ruinous over a trajectory.
    I'd keep TF32 off for anything that integrates past about 100 picoseconds, and step tight
    relaxations and vibrational-property runs up to float64. Treat precision as a per-workload knob
    whose acceptance test is the energy-drift curve, and set it in the request, not the global config.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Predict forces directly.</strong> Some models add a head that emits forces
    and skips the backward pass, roughly halving the step. Orb-v3 ships direct and conservative
    variants side by side; UMA and eSEN train a direct head internally but release conservative
    checkpoints only, so check what a family actually ships before you expose the option. The price
    is that predicted forces are no longer exactly the gradient of the predicted energy, nothing
    enforces energy conservation, and long MD drifts. For relaxations and high-throughput screening,
    where each structure gets a short burst of calls and nobody integrates for a nanosecond, direct
    forces are close to free. For long dynamics they are a liability. Expose the choice per workload
    rather than baking a default.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Strict locality for scale-out.</strong> Message passing widens what an atom
    sees: after each interaction block, information has traveled one more cutoff. Four blocks at 6
    ångströms means every atom's energy depends on atoms 24 ångströms away. On one GPU that costs
    nothing. Across nodes it is the whole bill. Domain decomposition, which is how LAMMPS
    parallelizes, gives each rank a spatial region plus a halo of ghost atoms copied from neighboring
    ranks, and the halo must be as thick as the receptive field. Thick halos mean each rank spends
    its time shipping copies instead of computing. Allegro is built strictly local: every atom's
    energy contribution depends only on its own one-cutoff neighborhood, regardless of depth, so its
    halos stay thin and it scales across many nodes. For a billion-atom run, reach for Allegro before
    any deep message-passing model. The model choice is the systems choice.
  </p>

