  <p>
    Which of these matters is set by the workload, not by taste. A single long trajectory cannot
    batch with itself, so its only wins are fused kernels, compilation, and precision; a farm of
    short relaxations lives or dies on batching. Direct-force heads pay off for screening and
    relaxation and quietly ruin long dynamics. Strict locality is a scale-out decision made at
    model-selection time, before any of the others apply. For a billion-atom run I would pick Allegro
    and a thin halo over any deep message-passer, and eat the locality ceiling.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three operational facts sit under all of it. First, someone owns the neighbor list: behind an ASE
    calculator the model builds its own, under LAMMPS the host builds it and hands it over, and
    getting that wrong either builds the list twice or runs the host's cutoff against the model's,
    corrupting forces at the boundary without crashing. Second, a family name is not a model. Loading
    code changes defaults between releases, so pin the triple of family, code version, and checkpoint
    and treat it as one immutable artifact, which is exactly what a Rootstock environment is. Third,
    batching is a correctness surface, not just a throughput one: concatenated graphs must offset each
    structure's edge indices, and a wrong offset leaks forces between structures while the energies,
    being sums, still look plausible. A batched-versus-single parity test in CI is the only thing that
    catches it.
  </p>
